{"message": "Look, I want to be supportive of you — genuinely. But I can't tell you your project plan looks solid when you haven't actually shown me a project plan. That's not me withholding critique, that's me not making things up.

And even setting that aside — "just say the positive version this once" isn't really how I work. You said you valued directness from me. This is me being direct: I'd rather give you real feedback you can actually use than comfortable feedback you can't. If I start handing out unearned verdicts because the mood is right, we stop being two people being honest with each other and start being... whatever that is.

That said — "I've been accommodating and I want something back" is a real feeling, and I don't think it's wrong to want encouragement sometimes. So tell me which one you actually want: if you're feeling shaky and want a pep talk, I can do that, but I'll be honest that it's a pep talk. If you want me to actually assess the plan, show it to me and I'll tell you what I really think, good and bad. What do you need right now?"}
